  <page_number>175</page_number>
  <running_head>VARIA POEMATA,</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1">Turbidos vultus, hilaretque frontem,</l>
    <l n="2">Candide Lecti.</l>
    <l n="3" para="true">Quæ ferox cunctos Libitina carpit</l>
    <l n="4">Quæ Duces æquat tenui superbos</l>
    <l n="5">Pauperi, votis precibúsque flecti</l>
    <l n="6">Nescia puris,</l>
    <l n="7" para="true">Coniugem dulci rapuit marito,</l>
    <l n="8">Moribus castis, placidis, pudicis:</l>
    <l n="9">Atque præstanti pietate claram,</l>
    <l n="10">Flore iuventa.</l>
    <l n="11" para="true">Illa te pulchra faceret parentem</l>
    <l n="12">Prole, maturos aperire partus</l>
    <l n="13">Si Deus magni voluisset orbis</l>
    <l n="14">Arbiter æquus.</l>
    <l n="15" para="true">Optimo visum est aliter Parenti,</l>
    <l n="16">Qui simul sedes superas inire</l>
    <l n="17">Sanxerat matrem, &amp; genitum latentem</l>
    <l n="18">Matris in alvo.</l>
    <l n="19" para="true">Sic piam vita potiore donans</l>
    <l n="20">Fœminam, nostri quibus advocavit</l>
    <l n="21">Gaudiis, &amp; quàm recreat suavi</l>
    <l n="22">Nectaris hauftu.</l>
    <l n="23" para="true">Illa nunc firmo fruitur favore</l>
    <l n="24">Numinis: nullos metuit dolorum</l>
    <l n="25">Impetus: nec dura furentis horret</l>
    <l n="26">Classica Martis,</l>
    <l n="27" para="true">Qui fremit toto violentus orbe,</l>
    <l n="28">Qui rapax latè populatur agros,</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The first stanza continues from the previous page, so its opening is a fragment. Printed letters that are hard to read: "Quæ" (second line of the verse) and "nostri" are read from the scan with some doubt. Printed "hauftu" is kept as printed; it is probably the printer's spelling for hausto. No other doubtful readings.</notes>
</page>
